\DOC add_type_simps

\TYPE {add_type_simps : string -> unit}

\SYNOPSIS
Declare the distinctness and injectivity of a type into the simp set.

\DESCRIBE
A call {add_type_simps "ty"} declares the two theorems that a proof about
the datatype {ty} always wants: that its constructors are distinct and
that they are injective. HOL Light proves both when the type is defined
and keeps them in {distinctness_store} and {injectivity_store}, so this
is a lookup and not a proof.

A type with one constructor has no distinctness theorem and a type whose
constructors take no arguments has no injectivity theorem; whichever
exists is declared and the other is passed over.

\FAILURE
Never fails, not even for a name that is no type.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # add_type_simps "exp";;
  val it : unit = ()

  # SIMPS_CONV [] `Num 3 = Call Nil`;;
  val it : thm = |- Num 3 = Call Nil <=> false
}

\SEEALSO
add_simps, distinctness, injectivity, simps.

\ENDDOC
